if(sixFrenchGet('northern-france')){
 const c=sixFrenchGet('northern-france');
 c.canon='The continuing État français retains the French name and constitutional inheritance under German strategic supervision. Its postwar politics, provincial geography, symbols, controlled rearmament, and the 1974 war form distinct parts of its institutional history.';
 sixFrenchSection('northern-france','territory','Territory and frontiers',"<p>The northern state retains Paris, Normandy, Picardie, Champagne, Orléanais, the French Loire country, Nancy, Dijon, and Besançon. Amiens, Abbeville, and Le Crotoy remain French. Lille, Arras, Calais, and Dunkirk belong to Belgium; Alsace-Moselle belongs to Germany. Historical five-department Brittany, including Nantes and Saint-Nazaire, is a separate German-guaranteed state.</p><p>Poitiers and Châtellerault lie in Occitania. The southern frontier separates Tours, Chinon, Angers, Saumur, Le Mans, and Laval from the Occitan western provinces. Northern cartography preserves inherited regional labels even after the <a href='french-provincial-settlement.html'>1948 renaming and 1952 Occitan reform</a>.</p>");
 const history=c.sections?.find(s=>s.id==='history');
 if(history){history.html=history.html.replace('In this timeline, Axis victory prevents the republican liberation that in real history repudiated Vichy\'s constitutional acts.','Axis victory prevents a republican liberation and repudiation of Vichy’s constitutional acts.').replace('the nine-region client state commonly called Northern France or France proper','the reduced client state commonly called Northern France or France proper');}
 sixFrenchSection('northern-france','postwar-politics','Postwar political organization',"<p>In 1947 the seat of government and central ministries moved from Vichy to Paris. Pétain accepted the end of hostilities and delegated practical authority without celebrating permanent partition. He retired at Vichy and died in 1951. Administrative conservatives associated with Pierre-Étienne Flandin supplied governmental continuity; Jacques Doriot and the PPF tradition supplied mass mobilization.</p><p>The army, strategic police, foreign affairs, and German-facing economic institutions remained partly beyond Doriot’s control. De Gaulle returned under safe conduct to Colombey as a private man, rejecting the legitimacy of partition without holding office in the continuing French State.</p>");
 sixFrenchSection('northern-france','rearmament','Rearmament and the war of 1974',"<p>German–Italian rivalry allowed a German-equipped and trained French army directed toward the Occitan frontier. French planners regarded this as an opening for restoration. Berlin intended a defensive client and retained the industrial, logistical, intelligence, and higher air-defense dependencies needed to prevent an independent French war.</p><p>France opened the <a href='french-occitan-war-1974.html'>war of 9–23 September 1974</a> down the Saône toward Mâcon. A Poitevin-led coalition advanced toward Tours. Germany and the Latin patrons imposed a ceasefire and restored the frontier without deliberately fighting one another. The French expectation that an offensive could force Germany into the war failed.</p>");
 sixFrenchSection('northern-france','post-1974','Politics after 1974',"<p>During the next two or three years, the military explored routes to victory against Occitania and its Latin protectors without a German intervention. The result weakened restoration as a practical governing program. Hard restorationists remained, alongside national realists seeking autonomy within the remaining state, continental technocrats favoring contracts and integration, and reconciliationists supporting normalization.</p><p>Public culture retained the anthem, Revolution, Napoleon, literature, museums, and a French civilization larger than the state’s borders. German procurement employment, factory relationships, and professional respect made political hostility coexist with a material interest in continental stability.</p>");
 sixFrenchSection('northern-france','food-demography','Food and demography',"<p>The <a href='french-provisioning-panic.html'>provisioning panic of 1947–1950</a> reflected disrupted trade and distribution rather than a permanent loss of staple self-sufficiency. Beauce, Normandy, Picardie, Orléanais, and the Loire country remained productive. Mechanization and continental trade resolved the immediate problem, while small-family prudence and demographic pessimism outlived it.</p>");
 c.facts=c.facts.filter(x=>x[0]!=='Constituent regions');
 sixFrenchFact('northern-france','Western frontier','Poitiers and Châtellerault are Occitan; Nantes and Saint-Nazaire are Breton');
 sixFrenchFact('northern-france','War of 1974','9–23 September; frontier restored');
 sixFrenchRelated('northern-france',[sixFrenchGeography,sixFrenchWarLink,sixFrenchIndustry,sixFrenchDiplomacy,sixFrenchLink('french-political-factions','French Political Factions'),sixFrenchLink('northern-french-political-culture','Northern French Political Culture'),sixFrenchLink('charles-de-gaulle','Charles de Gaulle')]);
}
if(sixFrenchGet('occitania')){
 const c=sixFrenchGet('occitania');
 c.lead="<strong>Occitania</strong> is a sovereign confederation of nineteen southern French provinces and a core power of the Latin Bloc. It emerged from the Italian-administered Southern French Provinces after 1947. Italy supplied the initial security, corporate, and financial framework; Spain and Catholic institutions encouraged provincial liberty, aristocratic obligation, and a separate public culture. Toulouse is the principal diplomatic seat. Provincial governments retain substantial political, military, and industrial authority.";
 c.canon='The confederation combines common foreign relations, currency, communications, and interoperability with provincial government and armed forces. The territorial settlement of 1947, renaming of 1948, nineteen-province reform of 1952, and defense in 1974 shaped its institutions.';
 sixFrenchSection('occitania','territory','Territory and provinces',"<p>The <a href='french-provincial-settlement.html'>1952 settlement</a> established Vendée, Poitou, Charentes, Marche, Limousin, Auvergne, Lyonnais, Dauphiné, Aquitaine, Garonne, Rouergue, Gascogne, Toulousain, Béarn, Pays Basque, Foix, Roussillon, Languedoc, and Provence. The provinces contain Occitan, Poitevin–Saintongeais, Arpitan, Catalan, Basque, and French-speaking communities. State Occitan exonyms and provincial self-names can differ.</p><p>Poitiers and Châtellerault are Poitevin. Saint-Étienne and Saint-Chamond are Lyonnais. Grenoble is Dauphinois; Savoy is Italian. The Basque province centers on Bayonne and the three northern Basque territories. Agen, Montauban, Cahors, Marmande, Gourdon, Figeac, Villeneuve-sur-Lot, and Castelsarrasin belong to Garona. Nice remains in Provence. Nantes and Saint-Nazaire belong to Brittany.</p>");
 sixFrenchSection('occitania','government','Government',"<p>The provinces collectively constitute the state. Toulouse houses the principal diplomatic institutions and common bodies, but it does not govern Lyonnais or Poitou as Paris governs a northern administrative region. Provincial estates, municipalities, dioceses, universities, professional bodies, industrial institutions, and notable households possess separate authority. Historical titles convey duty and precedence without automatically making a province the property of a duke.</p><p>A common layer coordinates foreign relations, currency, strategic communications, intelligence exchange, infrastructure, and interoperability. Provincial consent remains necessary for the transfer of provincial military formations and much industrial expenditure. Antoine de Lévis-Mirepoix became a founding elder and public spokesman of the Council of Provinces. No ruling monarchy was created.</p>");
 sixFrenchSection('occitania','confederal-defense','Provincial defense and 1974',"<p>The <a href='provincial-host-system.html'>provincial military system</a> maintains complete local defense forces with different emphases. Common standards and regular exercises enable cooperation. In 1974, Lyonnais and Dauphiné fought the eastern defense while Poitou, Charentes, and Vendée formed a western coalition. Marche held its prepared frontier; Auvergne released part of its mobile reserve after identifying the French main effort.</p><p>The <a href='french-occitan-war-1974.html'>war</a> ended with the frontier restored. Its aftermath strengthened stockpiles, defensive preparation, liaison, and agreed movement procedures. The provinces retained their authority. Spain’s western reinforcement and Italy’s eastern firebreak demonstrated a Latin guarantee wider than an exclusively Italian relationship.</p>");
 sixFrenchSection('occitania','industrial-plurality','Provincial industry',"<p>MAS in Lyonnais, MAC in Poitou, MAT in Limousin, the Roanne armor works, Turbomeca near Pau, Toulouse aerospace, and the coastal industrial systems provide distinct technical centers. Provinces maintain arsenals, procurement and test institutions, repair works, ammunition facilities, and licensed-production capacity. Common standards join this dispersed industry without requiring common ownership.</p><p>Provincial diplomacy follows the same pattern. Provence has strong Italian links, the Pyrenean and western provinces have Spanish relationships, Aquitaine looks toward Atlantic commerce, and Vendée maintains close contacts with Brittany. Toulouse coordinates these connections rather than replacing them.</p>");
 sixFrenchFact('occitania','Political form','Confederation of provinces; Catholic and corporative public order');
 sixFrenchFact('occitania','Provinces','19 after the 1952 reform');
 sixFrenchFact('occitania','Armed forces','Provincial armies and common coordinating services');
 sixFrenchFact('occitania','Constitutional office-holders','Lévis-Mirepoix: founding Council of Provinces leadership; detailed later succession unspecified');
 sixFrenchRelated('occitania',[sixFrenchGeography,sixFrenchWarLink,sixFrenchIndustry,sixFrenchDiplomacy,sixFrenchLink('antoine-de-levis-mirepoix','Antoine de Lévis-Mirepoix'),sixFrenchLink('occitan-heraldry','Occitan Heraldry'),sixFrenchLink('occitan-computing','Occitan Computing'),sixFrenchLink('occitan-extraterrestrial-communities','Occitan Extraterrestrial Communities')]);
}
if(sixFrenchGet('four-frances')){
 sixFrenchSection('four-frances','partition','The postwar partition',"<p>The État français retains Paris and the northern state apparatus. Occitania receives the southern territories, including Bordeaux, Poitiers, and Châtellerault. Brittany includes historical five-department Brittany and the whole Nantes–Saint-Nazaire industrial system. Belgium receives Nord and Pas-de-Calais; Amiens, Abbeville, and Le Crotoy remain French. Germany annexes Alsace-Moselle and Luxembourg; Italy holds Savoy while Grenoble and Nice remain Occitan.</p><p>The <a href='french-provincial-settlement.html'>1948 renaming and 1952 reorganization</a> give Occitania nineteen provinces. The historical term Four Frances includes the territorial northern, Occitan, and Breton states and the non-territorial underground claim. Diplomatic discussion may instead compare the four recognized governments of Paris, Toulouse, Rennes, and Brussels because Belgium holds former French territory.</p>");
 sixFrenchGet('four-frances').facts=[['Northern state','Continuing État français'],['Occitan provinces','19 after 1952'],['Brittany','Five-department historical territory; Nantes and Saint-Nazaire'],['Belgian transfer','Nord and Pas-de-Calais'],['German annexations','Alsace-Moselle and Luxembourg'],['Italian annexation','Savoy'],['Non-territorial claim','Underground France']];
 sixFrenchSection('four-frances','1974','The war of 1974',"<p>French restorationism produced a two-front war from 9 to 23 September 1974. The ceasefire restored the territorial settlement. The war made Occitan and Breton statehood harder to dismiss as temporary, exposed the limits of German guarantees, and reduced foreign sympathy for territorial revision by force.</p>");
 sixFrenchRelated('four-frances',[sixFrenchGeography,sixFrenchWarLink,sixFrenchDiplomacy,sixFrenchLink('french-political-factions','French Political Factions')]);
}
if(sixFrenchGet('belgium')){
 const c=sixFrenchGet('belgium');c.canon='The protectorate preserves Belgian institutions, nationality, and monarchy under German reserved powers. Nord and Pas-de-Calais form its French territorial acquisition; eastern cantons pass to Germany. Later cabinet and dynastic succession details are not uniform across the period.';
 sixFrenchSection('belgium','status','Constitutional position',"<p>The <a href='belgian-royal-settlement.html'>Royal Settlement</a> preserves Leopold III and a functioning monarchy beneath German protectorate authority. The Crown arbitrates domestic linguistic, religious, ministerial, and territorial questions. Ministries, courts, municipalities, parliamentary and corporative institutions retain ordinary administration.</p><p>Germany reserves decisive defense, foreign-policy, security, infrastructure, and strategic industrial powers. Belgium remains Belgian by nationality and institutional identity while its military and economic dependencies become German.</p>");
 sixFrenchSection('belgium','territory','Territory',"<p>Belgium receives Nord and Pas-de-Calais, including Lille, Arras, Calais, and Dunkirk. Amiens, Abbeville, and Le Crotoy remain in French Picardie. Eupen, Malmedy, and Sankt Vith pass to Germany. The enlarged French-speaking population creates a domestic constitutional question as well as a French territorial grievance.</p>");
 sixFrenchSection('belgium','dutch-migration','Dutch migration',"<p>As the Netherlands moves toward incorporation and Germanization, Dutch migrants seek the separate institutions of Flanders. Publishers, teachers, clergy, lawyers, merchants, engineers, and families strengthen Antwerp and other Dutch-speaking centers. Belgium permits cultural refuge while preventing an organized anti-Reich government-in-exile. The monarchy gains value as evidence that national dependence need not mean national absorption.</p>");
 sixFrenchFact('belgium','Transferred territory','Nord and Pas-de-Calais');sixFrenchFact('belgium','Monarchy','Preserved through the Royal Settlement');
 sixFrenchRelated('belgium',[sixFrenchDiplomacy,sixFrenchLink('belgian-royal-settlement','Belgian Royal Settlement'),sixFrenchLink('dutch-migration-to-belgium','Dutch Migration to Belgium'),sixFrenchLink('fabrique-nationale','Fabrique Nationale')]);
}
if(sixFrenchGet('brittany')){
 const c=sixFrenchGet('brittany');c.canon='The Breton state retains historical five-department territory, Rennes as capital, Nantes and Saint-Nazaire, Catholic and regional institutions, and German strategic guarantees and facilities.';
 sixFrenchSection('brittany','partition','Creation in the French partition',"<p>The settlement of 1947 creates a separate Breton state under German guarantee. Its territory includes historical five-department Brittany, with the whole Nantes and Saint-Nazaire industrial and port system. Nantes remains a large French- and Gallo-speaking city within a Breton national project.</p><p>Raymond Delaporte’s collaboration-era nationalists supplied founding leadership. Yann Fouéré developed constitutional and foreign-policy arguments for autonomy, and Joseph Martray organized the developmental politics that made the state economically viable.</p>");
 sixFrenchSection('brittany','economy','Economy and development',"<p>Agriculture, fisheries, food processing, commercial shipping, ship repair, electrical and marine equipment, light and medium engineering, tourism, and pilgrimage support the civilian economy. Nantes and Saint-Nazaire add heavy mechanics, locomotive expertise, major yards, engines, and skilled labor; Rennes retains an arsenal nucleus.</p><p>Developmental policy emphasizes roads, electrification, refrigeration, cooperatives, technical education, ports, and local employment. German credit and contracts sustain investment while reserved naval facilities limit control. The principal threshold is a functioning civilian state, not an independent strategic arms industry.</p>");
 sixFrenchSection('brittany','foreign','Foreign relations',"<p>Brittany’s Catholic, Celtic, maritime, and regional identity supports close relations with Ireland. Those contacts also provide a practical route into German continental trade and defensive procurement. Britain distinguishes Breton cultural recognition from German use of Atlantic naval and air facilities.</p><p>Relations with neighboring Vendée are important within Occitan provincial diplomacy. The war of 1974 creates relief at avoiding involvement and concern that a wider crisis could require fighting a familiar neighbor. Recognition hardens while German strategic dependency continues.</p>");
 c.facts=c.facts.filter(x=>x[0]!=='Disputed frontier');sixFrenchFact('brittany','Territory','Historical five-department Brittany, including Nantes and Saint-Nazaire');
 sixFrenchRelated('brittany',[sixFrenchDiplomacy,sixFrenchGeography,sixFrenchLink('raymond-delaporte','Raymond Delaporte'),sixFrenchLink('yann-fouere','Yann Fouéré'),sixFrenchLink('joseph-martray','Joseph Martray')]);
}
if(sixFrenchGet('philippe-petain')){
 const c=sixFrenchGet('philippe-petain');
 c.canon=c.canon.replace(/Pétain's conduct after the divergence, duration of authority, retirement, residence, death, funeral, and place in the eventual succession remain open\.<sup[^>]*>\[e\]<\/sup>/,'');
 sixFrenchSection('philippe-petain','later-war','Later war and retirement',"<p>Pétain remained the symbolic head of the Vichy-based French State through the later war, with age reducing his daily role. In 1947 he accepted the end of hostilities and the transfer or delegation of practical authority to the reconstructed Paris administration. He avoided personally endorsing permanent French partition.</p><p>The ministries and seat of government moved to Paris. Pétain retired at Vichy as Marshal of France and former chief of state. He received honors, attended Mass, and received controlled veterans’ visits under respectful political supervision. He did not become an Occitan nationalist or head of government. He died in 1951.</p>");
 sixFrenchFact('philippe-petain','Later transfer and death','Retirement at Vichy; died 1951');sixFrenchFact('philippe-petain','Died','1951');
 sixFrenchRelated('philippe-petain',[sixFrenchLink('charles-de-gaulle','Charles de Gaulle'),sixFrenchLink('french-political-factions','French Political Factions')]);
}
const sixFrenchProvincialRows=[
 ['Lyonnais','Mobile combined-arms defense and counterattack on the Saône; armor, tactical aviation, MAS, Saint-Chamond, and Roanne manufacturing; export success competes with war reserves.'],
 ['Dauphiné','Mountain and mechanized forces, helicopters, engineers, and transit; first reinforcement of Lyonnais and conduit for Italian assistance through Savoy.'],
 ['Auvergne','Defense in depth, mobile reserves, artillery, maintenance, and transport; retains its short frontier while releasing part of the reserve eastward.'],
 ['Marche','Prepared northern positions, territorial infantry, obstacles, artillery, anti-armor defense, and dispersed depots; difficult to transfer wholesale.'],
 ['Poitou','Complete heavy combined arms with additional armor, engineering, bridging, artillery, and deep ammunition stocks; MAC at Châtellerault.'],
 ['Vendée','Mobile territorial and mechanized defense with coastal security; assists Poitou while retaining forces opposite Brittany.'],
 ['Charentes','Flexible mechanized support, artillery, engineers, ordnance, maintenance, coastal and air defense; hinge between Poitou and Aquitaine.'],
 ['Limousin','Training, replacement formations, ammunition, repair, and second-echelon reserves; MAT at Tulle.'],
 ['Aquitaine','Atlantic naval headquarters, anti-submarine warfare, maritime patrol, convoy protection, ports, and coastal air defense.'],
 ['Garonne','Transport, signals, engineers, fuel, bridging, and a balanced mobile reserve connecting the western provinces.'],
 ['Gascogne','Mechanized mobility, reconnaissance, aviation support, Pyrenean approaches, and western reserve.'],
 ['Toulousain','Balanced field forces, aerospace, air defense, reconnaissance, communications, and the common institutions at Toulouse.'],
 ['Rouergue','Rugged-country training, artillery, motorized infantry, ammunition, and reserve mobilization in depth.'],
 ['Languedoc','Flexible reinforcement, airfields, logistics, mechanized infantry, and artillery along the southern east–west corridors.'],
 ['Provence','Mediterranean ports, repair, naval aviation, air defense, transport, and Italian logistical connections.'],
 ['Béarn','Mountain defense, aviation training, local combined arms, and the Pau–Bordes aerospace-engine industry.'],
 ['Pays Basque','Territorial and coastal defense, Bayonne security, patrol craft, and local air defense.'],
 ['Foix','Mountain infantry, scouts, engineers, and local air surveillance of Pyrenean approaches.'],
 ['Roussillon','Spanish transit, territorial defense, engineers, transport, coastal security, and air defense.']
];
const sixFrenchProvincialTable="<div class='table-wrap'><table class='wiki-table'><thead><tr><th>Province</th><th>Military emphasis</th></tr></thead><tbody>"+sixFrenchProvincialRows.map(r=>'<tr><td>'+r[0]+'</td><td>'+r[1]+'</td></tr>').join('')+'</tbody></table></div>';
const sixFrenchArmyOrganization="<p>The land forces belong to the provinces. Every medium or large province maintains infantry, artillery, engineers, reconnaissance, logistics, communications, reserves, air defense, and some armor. Local priorities influence the marginal investment; they do not make a provincial force incapable of either attack or defense.</p><p>Common staffs coordinate planning, intelligence, strategic communications, airspace, interoperability, and emergency rail priorities. Provincial authorities retain mobilization, procurement, and ownership of their formations. Transfers to another theater require consent and agreed command arrangements. Regular exercises and common interfaces allow the allied provincial armies to cooperate.</p>";
const sixFrenchArmyMobilization="<p>Opening defense relies on what a province already has. During the first week, standing neighboring agreements bring reinforcements such as Dauphiné to Lyonnais and Charentes to Poitou. Wider redistribution follows political consent, transport allocation, supply arrangements, and an agreed command. The two-week war of 1974 ended before a fully integrated confederal wartime system could develop.</p>";
for(const slug of ['occitan-army','provincial-host-system']){
 const c=sixFrenchGet(slug);if(!c)continue;
 const end='<'+'!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->';
 if(c.canon?.includes('<'+'!-- altwwii-writer-block:start')){
  const start=c.canon.indexOf(' The eight-formation roster');
  const alt=c.canon.indexOf(' The Provincial Host System title');
  const cut=start>=0?start:alt;
  if(cut>=0)c.canon=c.canon.slice(0,cut)+end+' The nineteen provinces retain military authority within common planning and interoperability arrangements.';
 }
 if(slug==='occitan-army'){
  c.lead='The Occitan Army is the collective land force of the provinces of Occitania. Provincial guards developed through regiment and division stages into complete combined-arms institutions. Common staffs, exercises, signals, and equipment standards support cooperation among forces whose mobilization and deployment remain provincial decisions.';
  sixFrenchSection(slug,'organization','Organization',sixFrenchArmyOrganization);
  sixFrenchSection(slug,'politics','Provincial and common authority',sixFrenchArmyOrganization+sixFrenchArmyMobilization);
  sixFrenchSection(slug,'industry','Equipment and industry',"<p><a href='mas-saint-etienne.html'>MAS</a> in Lyonnais, <a href='mac-chatellerault.html'>MAC</a> in Poitou, <a href='mat-tulle.html'>MAT</a> in Limousin, and the <a href='roanne-armaments-school.html'>Roanne manufacturing school</a> provide distinct armaments institutions. Toulouse and Bordeaux aerospace, Berliet, turbines, electronics, chemicals, ports, and provincial arsenals sustain domestic equipment and Latin imports. Standard interfaces permit provincial variants without requiring a separate supply system for every force.</p>");
  sixFrenchSection(slug,'air','Aviation',"<p>Major formations maintain habitual reconnaissance, liaison, close-support, and point-defense relationships. Provincial air elements share common technical certification, airspace coordination, ammunition, servicing, and communications. That common framework does not give Toulouse unrestricted ownership of provincial aircraft or formations.</p>");
 }else{
  c.lead='The Provincial Host System is the Occitan organization of complete provincial armed forces within a common defense compact. It combines territorial recruitment, local government and industrial support, common standards, and regular combined exercises. The nineteen provinces retain different military priorities and cooperate through agreed commands.';
  sixFrenchSection(slug,'principle','Organizing principle',sixFrenchArmyOrganization);
  sixFrenchSection(slug,'table','The nineteen provincial military systems',sixFrenchProvincialTable);
  sixFrenchSection(slug,'standards','Common standards',"<p>Ammunition, radio protocols, fuels, fittings, electrical systems, artillery data, railway transport, aircraft servicing, and Latin interoperability rules join separate provincial arsenals and customers. The common layer certifies interfaces rather than taking ordinary ownership of every formation or plant.</p>");
  sixFrenchSection(slug,'control','Command and mobilization',sixFrenchArmyMobilization+"<p>The provincial forces are public institutions, not private armies belonging to dukes. Provincial government itself is constrained by public institutions, budgets, military agreements, and common defense obligations. Common staffs do not obtain a routine power to strip Poitou of its army because Lyonnais requests reinforcement.</p>");
  sixFrenchSection(slug,'comparison','Foreign assessment',"<p>French observers frequently described Occitania as if Toulouse commanded one centralized army. The war of 1974 exposed the error: pressure on Lyonnais did not automatically require Poitou to abandon a successful western offensive. Provincial observers remembered separate experiences inside the same war.</p>");
 }
 sixFrenchSection(slug,'1974','The war of 1974',"<p><a href='french-occitan-war-1974.html'>From 9 to 23 September 1974</a>, Lyonnais defended the Saône corridor with Dauphinois and later Auvergnat assistance. Poitou advanced toward Tours with Charentes and Vendée. Marche retained its prepared frontier; deeper provinces supplied reserves, communications, repair, ammunition, and transit.</p><p>The settlement preserved provincial sovereignty. Postwar changes strengthened war stocks, liaison, agreed reinforcement plans, fortifications, and common interfaces. They did not create a centralized national army.</p>");
 sixFrenchFact(slug,'Principal formations','Provincial forces of nineteen provinces; older formation names are not an exhaustive constitutional roster');
 sixFrenchFact(slug,'Superior authority','Provincial governments; common coordinating staffs');
 sixFrenchFact(slug,'National controls','Common standards and agreements; provincial mobilization, procurement, and deployment');
 sixFrenchRelated(slug,[sixFrenchGeography,sixFrenchWarLink,sixFrenchIndustry]);
}
// Coherent dress addition requested by the source owner; no earlier roster is copied.
sixFrenchSection('occitan-army','dress','Dress and personal protection',"<p>The Modèle 1953 helmet, warm olive dress, and common and provincial insignia distinguish the provincial forces. Native camouflage standardized around 1970 remains unevenly issued in 1974. Fragmentation vests protect selected mechanized and specialist formations. The 1974 armor controversy leads to the Aquitanian-origin combat cuirass and wider modular protection in the later 1970s.</p>");
sixFrenchRelated('occitan-army',[sixFrenchLink('occitan-military-dress','Occitan Military Dress'),sixFrenchLink('occitan-heraldry','Occitan Heraldry'),sixFrenchLink('french-occitan-personal-armor','French and Occitan Personal Armor'),sixFrenchLink('occitan-combat-cuirass','Occitan Combat Cuirass')]);
const sixFrenchUnitSettings={
 'toulouse-mechanized-division':['Toulousain','balanced mechanized forces, aerospace-linked communications, air defense, reconnaissance, and support for the common institutions at Toulouse'],
 'rhodanian-armored-division':['Lyonnais','mobile armored defense and counterattack along the Saône, supported by the Lyonnais armaments system'],
 'bordeaux-guyenne-marine-division':['Aquitaine','Atlantic ports, maritime patrol, anti-submarine warfare, convoy protection, coastal defense, and marine responsibilities'],
 'provence-coastal-division':['Provence','Mediterranean ports, repair, naval aviation, air defense, and the Italian logistical connection'],
 'auvergne-mountain-division':['Auvergne','defense in depth, mobile reserves, artillery, transport, and maintenance, including rugged-country competence'],
 'gascon-cavalry-division':['Gascogne','mobile combined arms, reconnaissance, aviation support, and southwestern reserve responsibilities'],
 'bearn-foix-pyrenean-division':['Béarn and Foix','mountain defense and cooperation among separate Pyrenean provinces'],
 'limousin-marche-security-division':['Limousin and Marche','cooperation between Limousin reserve and repair depth and Marche’s prepared northern defense']
};
for(const [slug,[province,role]] of Object.entries(sixFrenchUnitSettings)){
 const c=sixFrenchGet(slug);if(!c)continue;
 c.lead='The '+c.title+' is a province-linked military formation associated with '+province+'. Its military tradition emphasizes '+role+'. The provincial military systems retain complete local defense and cooperate through common standards and agreed commands.';
 sixFrenchSection(slug,'command','Provincial command',sixFrenchArmyOrganization);
 sixFrenchSection(slug,'organization','Organization',"<p>The formation combines infantry, artillery, engineers, reconnaissance, communications, transport, logistics, air defense, and appropriate armor. Its regional emphasis does not remove its ability to fight both offensively and defensively. Provincial government owns the force; common standards and agreed theater commands enable combined operations.</p>");
 sixFrenchSection(slug,'national-responsibilities','Common responsibilities',"<p>Officer exchanges, shared technical education, exercises, communications, and agreed procurement interfaces support the confederal defense compact. Cooperation does not make the formation an unrestricted reserve that Toulouse can transfer by routine order.</p>");
 const old=c.sections.find(s=>s.id==='national-responsibilities');
 sixFrenchSection(slug,'record','The war of 1974',"<p>The provincial setting shapes its place in the <a href='french-occitan-war-1974.html'>war of 1974</a>. Lyonnais and Dauphiné form the eastern defensive coalition; Auvergne releases only part of its reserve; Poitou and its western allies advance toward Tours; Marche retains prepared defenses. Limousin, Tolosan, Aquitaine, Provence, and other interior or coastal institutions supply technical, logistical, reserve, and transit functions.</p>");
 sixFrenchFact(slug,'Province or provincial setting',province);sixFrenchFact(slug,'Command','Provincial authority under common defense agreements');
 sixFrenchRelated(slug,[sixFrenchGeography,sixFrenchWarLink]);
}
for(const slug of ['occitan-tank-lineage','occitan-mechanized-support-system','occitan-battlefield-integration']){
 const c=sixFrenchGet(slug);if(!c)continue;
 sixFrenchSection(slug,'provincial-production','Provincial production and customers',"<p>The <a href='roanne-armaments-school.html'>Roanne manufacturing school</a> supplies the principal armored production infrastructure. Lyonnais, Poitou, and other provincial customers retain different requirements and procurement priorities. Toulouse design and systems institutions remain part of the wider technical geography; common interfaces join provincial equipment without requiring a single owner or buyer.</p><p>Lyonnais favors mobile defense and counterattack along the Saône. Poitou maintains deep armored and engineer inventories for the Châtellerault gateway. Their different stocks helped produce sharply different French impressions of Occitan armor in 1974. Postwar policy increases minimum provincial war reserves and prevents export commitments from consuming mobilization stocks.</p>");
 sixFrenchRelated(slug,[sixFrenchWarLink,sixFrenchLink('roanne-armaments-school','Roanne Armaments School'),sixFrenchIndustry]);
}
for(const slug of Object.keys(sixFrenchUnitSettings)){
 const c=sixFrenchGet(slug);if(!c)continue;
 if(c.canon?.includes('<'+'!-- altwwii-writer-block:start')){
  const cut=c.canon.indexOf('</sup>');
  if(cut>=0)c.canon=c.canon.slice(0,cut+6)+'<'+'!-- altwwii-writer-block:end --> Provincial authority, agreed command, and common interoperability govern later service.';
 }
 for(const s of c.sections||[])s.html=s.html.replaceAll('National aviation authorities','Common aviation authorities').replaceAll('national General Staff controls','common staff coordinates').replaceAll('national responsibilities','common responsibilities');
}
if(sixFrenchGet('french-industrial-partition-1947')){
 sixFrenchSection('french-industrial-partition-1947','north','Northern France and continental integration',"<p>Northern France retains the Paris industrial region, Rouen, Orléans, Angers, Dijon, Nancy, Laon, and Châlons-sur-Marne. Poitiers and Châtellerault belong to Occitania; Nantes and Saint-Nazaire belong to Brittany. Immediate factory control follows territory while shares, patents, brands, debts, and pensions can remain disputed across the frontier.</p><p>French civilian industry remains useful and productive. Strategic arms manufacture is licensed, capped, or reserved to Germany. From the 1960s, German contracts increasingly employ French designers and plants inside supervised continental supply chains.</p>");
 sixFrenchSection('french-industrial-partition-1947','arsenals','State arsenals and heavy industry',"<p>MAS at Saint-Étienne is Lyonnais, MAC at Châtellerault Poitevin, and MAT at Tulle Limousin. Roanne supplies major armored production infrastructure. Northern AMX, APX, ARL, and private military departments retain design and prototype competence under production ceilings. Renault, SOMUA, Hotchkiss, and Panhard remain viable northern civilian firms even where engineers move south.</p><p>Rennes, Nantes, and Saint-Nazaire give Brittany ammunition, railway, heavy-mechanical, and shipbuilding capacities. Belgium retains FN, Cockerill, aerospace establishments, and the transferred Fives-Lille industrial system.</p>");
 sixFrenchRelated('french-industrial-partition-1947',[sixFrenchGeography,sixFrenchIndustry,sixFrenchLink('einheitspakt-standards','Einheitspakt Standards')]);
}
if(sixFrenchGet('french-aerospace-industrial-partition')){
 sixFrenchSection('french-aerospace-industrial-partition','postwar-institutions','Postwar institutions',"<p>The six nationalized regional aircraft companies had distinct plants and design staffs; SNCAN, SNCAO, SNCAM, SNCAC, SNCASO, and SNCASE were not six names for one firm. The territorial division assigned particular works and personnel to different governments. Gnome & Rhône remained in the northern Paris complex, while Hispano-Suiza’s Bois-Colombes and Tarbes assets generated cross-frontier claims.</p><p>Turbomeca at Bordes near Pau, Latécoère at Toulouse, and the Bordeaux, Marignane, Cannes, and other southern establishments supplied propulsion, airframes, controls, and technical staff. Italy retained prestige and procurement advantages. Occitan industry built strength in specialized systems, helicopters, guidance, electronics, and flight computing.</p><p>Later northern firms could contribute airframe structures, landing gear, pumps, actuators, instruments, and other portions of German projects. German supervision continued to prevent an independent French chain combining airframe, engine, radar, weapons, and mass production.</p>");
 sixFrenchRelated('french-aerospace-industrial-partition',[sixFrenchIndustry,sixFrenchGeography]);
}
if(sixFrenchGet('german-italian-rivalry')){
 sixFrenchSection('german-italian-rivalry','france','The French–Occitan frontier',"<p>Northern France regards the partition as temporary and the Occitan south as the first plausible object of restoration. Occitania fears abolition; Brittany and Belgium defend the territorial order that preserves their institutions. Berlin rearms France as a defensive client, while French planners hope the German–Italian split will permit reunion.</p><p>The <a href='french-occitan-war-1974.html'>war of 9–23 September 1974</a> tests that divergence. French eastern success and a Poitevin western advance approach visible patron firebreaks. Germany, Italy, and Spain stop the conflict and restore the frontier. Neither German nor Italian regulars deliberately fight the other’s forces.</p>");
 sixFrenchSection('german-italian-rivalry','crises','The principal war scares',"<p>The Balkan Missions Crisis exposes the danger of advisers and exclusive command influence. The Straits confrontation of about 1968–1969 brings contested passage, German and Italian movements, and Turkish control into direct proximity. The French–Occitan War is the bloodiest direct European client confrontation and the clearest attempt to exploit a patron’s defensive guarantee.</p><p>Germany and Italy have no useful project for conquering the other’s entire system. Wolfgang and Umberto repeatedly restrain clients and their own competing institutions while preserving rivalry over Bulgaria, Turkey, Switzerland, France, the Balkans, and overseas influence. Their private contact and accumulated crisis procedures eventually support the Verona Convention.</p>");
 sixFrenchRelated('german-italian-rivalry',[sixFrenchWarLink,sixFrenchDiplomacy]);
}
if(sixFrenchGet('great-power-escalation-ladder')){
 sixFrenchSection('great-power-escalation-ladder','french-occitan-example','The French–Occitan War of 1974',"<p>The <a href='french-occitan-war-1974.html'>French–Occitan War</a> is a Level 4 peripheral confrontation: interstate war for the clients and a bounded crisis for their patrons. Germany and Italy provide equipment, intelligence, reconnaissance, logistics, replacement support, and protected rear areas; Spanish reinforcement supports the western Latin system. Reversible information interference accompanies regular fighting between French and provincial Occitan forces.</p><p>Deliberate German–Italian regular combat would cross the Level 5 threshold. Visible rear formations and air defenses make that boundary geographic. The patrons end client offensives and restore the frontier before either can turn local success into a general strategic result.</p>");
 sixFrenchRelated('great-power-escalation-ladder',[sixFrenchWarLink]);
}
if(sixFrenchGet('straits-security-settlement')){
 sixFrenchSection('straits-security-settlement','1968-1969','The confrontation of 1968–1969',"<p>Disputed strategic transit through the Straits produces one of the principal German–Italian war scares. German claims under existing access arrangements meet Italian concern about the Balkan and eastern Mediterranean balance. Turkey delays authorization and mobilizes control of the channels while Italian and German forces move into positions from which further escalation is possible.</p><p>Wolfgang and Umberto settle the confrontation through direct contact. The resulting regime strengthens advance notice, mission and movement limits, restrictions on permanent foreign bases, and Turkey’s right to suspend strategic passage during a German–Italian confrontation. The episode contributes to the exercise-notification and emergency-consultation practices later written into Verona.</p>");
 sixFrenchRelated('straits-security-settlement',[sixFrenchWarLink]);
}
if(sixFrenchGet('verona-convention')){
 sixFrenchSection('verona-convention','1974-precedent','The 1974 precedent',"<p>The <a href='french-occitan-war-1974.html'>war of 1974</a> exposed the difference between protection from a client’s destruction and support for a client’s offensive ambitions. Paris tried to make German rear protection substitute for its own western reserve while pursuing Mâcon. Poitevin forces stopped before deliberately engaging Germans; Italy and Spain helped halt the provincial offensive.</p><p>That experience supplied a direct precedent for the convention’s restrictions on client aggression, emergency consultation, and offensive French reunification. Bulgaria and the Straits supplied earlier lessons about missions, exercise notice, access, and movement. The treaty regularized accumulated restraints while leaving the competing systems intact.</p>");
 sixFrenchRelated('verona-convention',[sixFrenchWarLink]);
}
if(sixFrenchGet('underground-france')){
 sixFrenchSection('underground-france','nationalist-traditions','Non-Communist nationalist traditions',"<p>La Rocque’s former PSF networks supply veterans, social associations, Catholics, and conservative nationalists who reject German submission without necessarily becoming Gaullists. De Gaulle’s private residence at Colombey offers a common national reference point outside the northern state’s offices. His death in 1970 produces mourning across successor frontiers.</p><p>These constituencies share opposition to permanent partition while disagreeing over the Republic, the Church, social policy, violence, and the legitimacy of the Paris government. Breton separatists and Occitan provincial nationalists do not become French unionists merely because they oppose excessive German or Italian control.</p>");
 sixFrenchRelated('underground-france',[sixFrenchLink('francois-de-la-rocque','François de La Rocque'),sixFrenchLink('charles-de-gaulle','Charles de Gaulle'),sixFrenchLink('french-political-factions','French Political Factions')]);
}
